rday; \n\nAn edition of Breaking Twitter (2023)\n\n# Breaking Twitter\n\n## Elon Musk and the Most Controversial Corporate Takeover in History\n\n## by Ben Mezrich\n\nBreaking Twitter\n\nBen Mezrich, Ben Mezrich\n\nAn edition of Breaking Twitter (2023)\n\n# Breaking Twitter\n\n## Elon Musk and the Most Controversial Corporate Takeover in History\n\n## by Ben Mezrich\n\n  * 1 Want to read\n\nThis edition doesn't have a description yet. Can you add one?\n\nPublish Date\n\n2023\n\nPublisher\n\nGrand Central Publishing\n\nLanguage\n\nEnglish\n\nEdition  | Availability\n--- | ---\nBreaking Twitter: Elon Musk and the Most Controversial Corporate Takeover in History 2024, Grand Central Publishing in English  | \n\n  * Learn More\nBreaking Twitter: Elon Musk and the Most Controversial Corporate Takeover in History 2024, Pan Macmillan in English  | \n\n  * Learn More\nBreaking Twitter: Elon Musk and the Most Controversial Corporate Takeover in History 2023, Pan Macmillan in English  | \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEditions of Burn Book: A Tech Love Story by Kara Swisher (https://www.goodreads.com/work/editions/197725600-burn-book-a-tech-love-story)\nciteturn349search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; \n\n# Burn Book > Editions\n\ncollapse details\n\n## by Kara Swisher First published February 27th 2024\n\nEditions\n\nShowing 1-18 of 18\n\nBurn Book: A Tech Love Story (Hardcover)\n\nPublished February 27th 2024 by Simon & Schuster\n\nFirst Edition, Hardcover, 305 pages\n\nAuthor(s):\n\nKara Swisher\n\nISBN:\n\n9781982163891 (ISBN10: 1982163895)\n\nASIN:\n\n1982163895\n\nEdition language:\n\nEnglish\n\nAverage rating:\n\n3.87 (12,724 ratings)\n\nBurn Book: A Tech Love Story (Kindle Edition)\n\nPublished February 27th 2024 by Simon & Schuster\n\nKindle Edition, 320 pages\n\nAuthor(s):\n\nKara Swisher\n\nASIN:\n\nB0CF72742R\n\nEdition language:\n\nEnglish\n\nAverage rating:\n\n4.26 (2,917 ratings)\n\nBurn Book: A tech love story (Kindle Edition)\n\nPublished February 27th 2024 by Piatkus\n\nUK, Kindle Edition, 336 pages\n\nAuthor(s):\n\nKara Swisher\n\nISBN:\n\n9780349442112 (ISBN10: 0349442118)\n\nASIN:\n\nB0CPLXB1N2\n\nEdition language:\n\nEnglish\n\nAverage rating:\n\n4.25 (217 ratings)\n\nBurn Book: A Tech Love Story (Audiobook)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBurn (https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/312914/burn-by-pontzer-herman/9780141990187)\nciteturn349search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; \nIn this book, he draws on twenty years of cutting-edge science to take a closer look at what happens to the energy we consume. Burn explores the ways in which metabolism controls every aspect of our health - from fertility to immune function - and reveals the truth about the dynamic system that sustains us.\nFilled with facts and memorable anecdotes, this book will change the way you think about food, exercise and what really matters for your health.\n\n\n> ## Pontzer's findings have huge implications for our attitudes to exercise, diet and public health\n> \n> Mark Webster, Sunday Times\n\n> ## Ground-breaking, fascinating, important . . . we were astounded\n> \n> Deliciously Ella, Delicious Ways to Feel Better\n\n> ## A fun, fast-paced, eye-opening, and innovative book that will revolutionize how you think about the energy that fuels your body and everything you do\n> \n> Daniel E. Lieberman, author of Exercised and The Story of the Human Body\n\n> ## Burn is science writing at its best... will reshape what you thought you knew about how our metabolisms work\n> \n> Alex Hutchinson, New York Times bestselling author of Endure\n\n> ## Burn will make you question what you think you know about metabolism and your waistline\n> \n> Stephan Guyenet, PhD, author of The Hungry Brain\n\n> ## Herman Pontzer is one of the most gifted science writers of our time\n> \n> Kelly McGonigal, PhD, author of The Joy of Movement\n\n## About Herman Pontzer\n\nHerman Pontzer is a professor of evolutionary anthropology and global health at Duke University. He is an internationally recognized researcher in human energetics and evolution. He is the author of Burn.\n\nLearn more\n\nDetails\n\n  * Imprint: Penguin\n  * Published: 02/03/2021\n  * ISBN: 9780141990187\n  * Length: 384 pages\n  * Price: £5.99\n\nAll editions\n\n  * Paperback 2022\n  * Ebook 2021\n  * Audio Download 2021--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBreaking Twitter by Ben Mezrich - Pan Macmillan (https://www.panmacmillan.com/authors/ben-mezrich/breaking-twitter/9781035032501)\nciteturn349search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nImage: Breaking Twitter book cover\n\n# Breaking Twitter\n\n## Ben Mezrich\n\nAudiobook\n\nBuy the book\n\n## Synopsis\n\nNew York Times bestselling author Ben Mezrich pulls back the curtain on the most volatile, complex, and bizarre corporate takeover in history: Elon Musk’s acquisition and subsequent occupation of Twitter.\n‘An excitable, high-octane account’ – Independent\nIn October 2022, Elon Musk marched through Twitter’s front doors after buying the digital giant for $44 billion. His takeover came with the promise of fundamental change, but nothing could prepare the company for the chaos to come – brutal mass firings, an exodus of advertisers and ‘blue-tick’ celebrities and a vicious battle for control.\nWith unique access to Twitter insiders and Musk’s confidants, this is the astonishing story from all sides. Why did Elon overhaul Twitter’s blue-tick system, and how did it lead to the near-collapse of the company’s revenue? Will Twitter – now X – survive? How has the constant negative press coverage affected Elon?\nWith a wealth of hidden details, Breaking Twitter gives ringside seats to one of the most dramatic and compelling business stories of our time.\n\n## Details\n\n09 November 2023\n\n544 pages\n\nWill Collyer\n\n9781035032501\n\nImprint: Macmillan Business\n\n## Reviews\n\n> “Uproarious . . . stimulating enough to keep even an unmedicated narcoleptic awake”Washington Times on The Accidental Billionaires\n\n> “An excitable, high-octane account”Independent, Independent\n\n> “Mezrich’s books are the sort of engrossing reads you pick up in the airport then find the blockbuster film on the plane”Evening Standard, Evening Standard--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBURN BOOK | Kirkus Reviews (https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/kara-swisher/burn-book-2/)\nciteturn349search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# BURN BOOK\n\n### A TECH LOVE STORY\n\nby Kara Swisher ‧ RELEASE DATE: Feb. 27, 2024\n\nSwisher for president.\n\nbookshelf\n\nshop now\n\namazon Barnes & Noble BN.com\n\nAn essential explanation of how tech has changed the world, from a truth-teller who has witnessed it at close range.\n\nYears ago, someone interviewing Swisher for an internship told her she was too confident. Her reply: “I’m not too confident, I’m fantastic. Or I will be.” It could be annoying, but most readers will agree with her. Swisher, who began as a journalist covering the rise of the internet and has since become a thought leader via conferences, publications, podcasts, and an opinion column in the New York Times, offers an account of her career that is fun to read, enlightening, and sometimes frightening. She’s been ahead of her time since the 1990s, when she supported a colleague in lodging a then-unheard-of sexual harassment complaint against talk show host John McLaughlin, and she has a clear talent for “scenario building, which is a fancy way of saying I’m a good guesser.” This is evident in her narration of the rise and fall of companies from AOL to Uber and the careers of “man-boys” like Sergey Brin, Mark Zuckerberg, Elon Musk, and many more. With regard to social media, Swisher writes that “engagement equals enragement,” and she predicted the Jan. 6 insurrection; in fact, she posed it as a hypothetical to see if Twitter would kick Trump off the platform. The tech world, she writes, is a “mirrortocracy, full of people who like their own reflection so much that they only saw value in those that looked the same,” people who “ignored issues of safety not because they were necessarily awful, but because they had never felt unsafe a day in their lives.” Though the book lives up to its title with scathing portraits of jerks and gross excesses, one of the most memorable aspects is Swisher’s deep respect for Steve Jobs, whom she laments as one of a kind.\n\nSwisher for president.\n\nPub Date: Feb. 27, 2024\n\nISBN: 9781982163891\n\nPage Count: 320\n\nPublisher: Simon & Schuster\n\nReview Posted Online: March 7, 2024\n\nKirkus Reviews Issue: April 15, 2024\n\nCategories:\n\nCURRENT EVENTS & SOCIAL ISSUES | SCIENCE & TECHNOLOGY | ISSUES & CONTROVERSIES | GENERAL CURRENT EVENTS & SOCIAL ISSUES\n\n  *   * shop now\n\namazon bookshop Barnes & Noble BN.com\n\nOur Verdict\n\n  * Image: Our Verdict\n  * GET IT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProfessor David Bernstein and New Book Featured in Wall Street Journal Interview | Antonin Scalia Law School (https://www.law.gmu.edu/news/2022/david_bernsteins_new_book_on_racial_classification_subject_of_feature_article_on_eve_of_supreme_court_affirmative_action_arguments)\nciteturn349search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n“They say, ‘Oh, no, no, you’re Asian.’ But this Asian thing is purely a statistical construct.”\n\nMr. Bernstein, 55, is the author of a recent book, Classified, that traces the haphazard codification of the federal government’s racial labels. “We created these classifications in 1977 in a very different America, right, that was primarily black-white,” he says. “Now we have all these other groups, and we have much more division within the groups, and we’ve barely changed them at all.”\n\nThe decisive player in the ‘70s was the Ad Hoc Committee on Racial and Ethnic Definitions, set up under the Federal Interagency Committee on Education. A task force with three interested federal workers—Mexican-American, Puerto Rican and Cuban-American—debated a Spanish-language label. The eventual result was a document with a title only a hardened bureaucrat could love: Statistical Policy Directive No. 15.\n\nIssued by the Office of Management and Budget in 1977, Directive 15 set definitions for the racial categories we mostly know today: white, black, Asian and Native American, with an ethnicity option for people of Hispanic heritage, who can be any race. In 1997 a Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islander grouping was split off. A complaint from Hawaiians, which echoes today, was that colleges treated them as Asians.\n\nIn a country that’s far more diverse five decades after Directive 15, its labels show up everywhere from college applications to clinical trials. The problem is that these blunt categories are arbitrary and historically contingent. “Asians are supposed to have descent from the original peoples of Asia, whatever that means,” Mr. Bernstein says. That grouping covers half the world’s population and a dizzying number of ethnicities. Yet according to the feds, Asia ends at the Pakistan border. Pakistani-Americans, like Japanese-Americans, are classified as Asian. Afghan-Americans are officially white.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBurn: The New Science of Human Metabolism - Herman Pontzer - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=rjGEzQEACAAJ)\nciteturn349search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; \n\n# Burn: The New Science of Human Metabolism\n\nImage: Front CoverHerman PontzerPenguin Random House, 2021 - Human evolution\"New research blows the lid off how we really burn calories, lose weight, and stay healthy\"--\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Burn: The New Science of Human Metabolism\nAuthor  | Herman Pontzer\nPublisher  | Penguin Random House, 2021\nISBN  | 0525541535, 9780525541530\nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBurn : new research blows the lid off how we really burn calories, lose weight, and stay healthy | WorldCat.org (https://search.worldcat.org/title/1238030817)\nciteturn349search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nImage: Front cover image for Burn : new research blows the lid off how we really burn calories, lose weight, and stay healthy\n\n# \n\nBurn : new research blows the lid off how we really burn calories, lose weight, and stay healthy\n\nAuthor:Herman Pontzer (Author)\n\nSummary:\"If you exercise and cut carbs you'll lose more weight, right? Wrong! Pontzer reveals how human metabolism really works: exercise does not increase our metabolism. Instead we burn calories within a very narrow range, no matter our activity level. Pontzer examines a variety of modern diet trends and explains how they succeed or fail: for shedding pounds, a calorie is a calorie. Exercise won't make us thinner, but it does keep our body systems functioning optimally. Discover how you really eat, move, and live\"-- adapted from jacket\n\n* * *\n\nPrint Book, English, 2021\n\nPublisher: Avery, an imprint of Penguin Random House, New York, 2021--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBreaking Twitter : : Elon Musk and the most controversial... (https://catalog.wake.gov/Record/779390)\nciteturn349search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n\n# Breaking Twitter: Elon Musk and the most controversial corporate takeover in history\n(Book)\n\nYour Rating: 0 stars\n\n[Input]\n\n* * *\n\nAverage user rating: 4 stars\n\nAuthor:\n\nMezrich, Ben, 1969-\n\nStatus:\n\nCary Re